Templated freeze-drying method, utilizing ice as the desired platform, confers excellent processibility for these MOF based composites.12 As a proof-of-concept, we apply five $\mathbf{MOF}@\mathbf{S}\mathbf{A}$ composites as basic units to assemble various hollow tubes (Figure 2). For instance, hollow tubes with conjugated structures are attained when connecting two parts of MOF@SA composites (Figure 2a,b). And when utilizing different sizes of templates, “cellular” hollow tubes with various holediameters (from $6.5~\mathrm{mm}$ to $1.0\ \mathrm{mm}\$ ) are produced (Figure 2d-f). Moreover, a piece of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}101@\mathrm{SA}$ based ice solid can be connected to one side of UIO$66@\mathrm{SA}$ hollow ice tube with trace amount of water. After freeze-drying, a hollow tube with one side sealed is manufactured (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ ). Beside, various double/threelayer hollow tube or membrane systems can be produced in a layer-by-layer fashion (Figure 2c,h,i,j). Taking (ZIF$8\ensuremath{+}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-101) $@$ SA-HT double-layer hollow tube for example, SEM images show the interface is tightly connected without any gap or crack (Figure 3d-g). This indicates the method is efficient to seamlessly combine different MOF systems together.
The robustness and diversity of these hollow tubes set fundamental basis for further applications. To study the feasibility of trapping certain analytes, we further fabricate ZIF-8 $\S(\varpi\mathrm{SA}$ into a hollow tube with one side sealed and utilize it in ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ treatment (Figure 3a,b). This hollow tube exhibits $96.8\%$ efficiency for ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ ( $20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF)) extraction and can be recycled for three times with remained structure integrity and porosity (Figure 3c, Figure S11 and S12). Additionally, a double-layer $(\mathrm{ZIF{-}}8\ +\ \mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MIL}{-}101)@\mathrm{SA}{-}$ HT with seamless integration of two MOF parts is produced for mixed-pollutants extraction (Figure 3d-g). As reported, ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ have strong interactions with ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ and methyl orange (MO).8a-d As a result, these two pollutants are successfully extracted from the mixed solution and high efficiency $\left(\mathrm{AsO}_{4}^{3-}\right.$ , $95.3\%$ and MO, $97.1\%$ ) are obtained compared with relative comparisons (Figure 3h-j, S21-22).
Despite the excellent properties in robustness and contaminant treatment, hollow tubes prepared through this protocol have poor water stability. SA, a water-soluble polymer composed of carboxylic sodium can be crossedlinked with metal ions like $\dot{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ (Figure S26a).13  After treating with $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution, water stable ZIF${8\textcircled{a}\mathrm{SA-HT}}$ $(50\ \mathrm{\wt\%})$ with remained robustness, morphology and ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ filtration efficiency is produced (Figure S26b,c, S27 and S28). Conducted under the same conditions, water stable $(Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\ \ +\ \mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL101) $@$ SA-HT shows high efficiency $(94.3\%$ and $96.8\%$ ) for mixed-pollutants (aqueous solution, ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ , $20\mathrm{mgL^{-}}$ and MO, $5~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ ) filtration compared with SA-HT $(28.2\%$ and $36.4\%$ ). In addition, the saturated capacity values of ZIF-8 $@$ SA-HT $(50~\mathrm{~wt\%})$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL$101\textcircled{a}\mathrm{SA-HT}$ $(50\mathrm{\wt\%})$ for $\mathrm{AsO}_{4}^{3-}$ and MO are calculated to be 51.9 and $152.7\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ respectively (based on the Langmuir adsorption isotherms), which are comparable to most of materials reported (Figure S23-25, Table S3-6). Considering the practical situations, we further set out to explore the filtration properties of the hollow tube for low-concentration mixed pollutants $\left(\mathrm{AsO}_{4}^{3-}\right.$ , $200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and MO, $200~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and it shows high filtration efficiency $(>96\%)$ even after seven cycles (Figure S29).
Furthermore, a “cellular” ZIF-8 $@$ SA-HT $(30\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ is produced for PM filtration (Figure 4b). PM is highly polar due to the existence of ions, water and other com
